Hi David,

> In your missing semantic section, I don=E2=80=99t fully understand how =
the 4th
point is improved by the KIP. It says start from earliest but with the
change it would start from application start time. Could you elaborate?

Thanks for the question. With by_start_time, the consumer seeks using =
its startup timestamp.=20
So after log truncation, any remaining records with timestamps at or =
after that startup time are still=20
reachable through the timestamp-based lookup, rather than being silently =
skipped.=20

By contrast, latest simply jumps to the current end of the partition and =
ignores all existing data.=20
The improvement here is that the timestamp-based approach preserves =
access to post-startup=20
data that survives truncation, whereas latest offers no such guarantee.=20=

I will update the KIP wording to make this clearer.

Best Regards,
Jiunn-Yang


> Chia-Ping Tsai <chia7712@apache.org> =E6=96=BC 2026=E5=B9=B43=E6=9C=886=E6=
=97=A5 =E4=B8=8B=E5=8D=885:12 =E5=AF=AB=E9=81=93=EF=BC=9A
>=20
> hi David
>=20
>> I was also considering this solution while we discussed in the jira. =
It
>> seems to work in most of the cases but not in all. For instance, =
let=E2=80=99s
>> imagine a partition created just before a new consumer joins or =
rejoins the
>> group and this consumer gets the new partition. In this case, the =
consumer
>> will have a start time which is older than the partition creation =
time.
>> This could also happen with the truncation case. It makes the =
behavior kind
>> of unpredictable again.
>=20
> Using a server-side timestamp means comparing the Group Coordinator's =
time against the Partition Leader's time (which is often a different =
node). Without strict clock synchronization in Kafka, this =
"happens-before" relationship remains fundamentally unpredictable.
>=20
>> Instead of relying on a local timestamp, one idea would to rely on a
>> timestamp provided by the server. For instance, we could define the =
time
>> since the group became non-empty. This would define the subscription =
time
>> for the consumer group. The downside is that it only works if the =
consumer
>> is part of a group.
>=20
> auto.offset.reset is strictly a client-level config. Consumers within =
the same group can intentionally use different policies. Tying this to a =
global group state feels like a semantic mismatch. A local timestamp =
aligns much better, similar to how by_duration operates.


