Hello chia,

Thanks for suggestion,

I like the hybrid direction. Building on that, I am considering =
separating this into two distinct strategies=20
rather than a single policy with automatic fallback. One would be =
by_start_time, which uses the consumer=E2=80=99s=20
local startup timestamp =E2=80=94 this is purely client-side and works =
well for dynamic consumers. The other would=20
be a group-level strategy that relies on server-side timestamps, where =
the reference point is when the group started.=20

These two strategies would not be mixed =E2=80=94 if the group-level =
strategy is configured and server-side timestamps=20
are not available, the consumer would fail explicitly rather than =
silently falling back. This keeps the behavior predictable=20
and avoids any hidden switching logic.

What do you think about this separation?

Best Regards,
Jiunn-Yang

> Chia-Ping Tsai <chia7712@apache.org> =E6=96=BC 2026=E5=B9=B43=E6=9C=8811=
=E6=97=A5 =E4=B8=8B=E5=8D=886:59 =E5=AF=AB=E9=81=93=EF=BC=9A
>=20
> I am thinking whether we could integrate both approaches. The logic =
would be as follows:
>=20
> 1. If both server-side timestamps exist, we leverage them to set the =
policy (Deterministic & safe for all).
>=20
> 2. If they don't, we fall back to using the local startup time as a =
best-effort approach.
>=20
> 3. However, if it is a static member and the server timestamps are =
missing, we throw an exception.
>=20
> Thoughts on this hybrid direction?
>=20
> On 2026/03/05 11:14:31 =E9=BB=83=E7=AB=A3=E9=99=BD wrote:
>> Hello everyone,=20
>>=20
>> I would like to start a discussion on KIP-1282: Prevent data loss =
during partition expansion for dynamically added partitions
>> <https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/x/mIY8G>
>>=20
>> This proposal aims to introduce a new auto.offset.reset policy =
by_start_time, anchoring the=20
>> offset reset to the consumer's startup timestamp rather than =
partition discovery time, to prevent=20
>> silent data loss during partition expansion.
>>=20
>> Best regards,
>> Jiunn-Yang


