sanghyeok An created KAFKA-20311:
------------------------------------

             Summary:   Cached metered key-value stores can deserialize old=
 values with the wrong headers when sendOldValues=3Dtrue
                 Key: KAFKA-20311
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-20311
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
            Reporter: sanghyeok An
            Assignee: sanghyeok An


=C2=A0In the cached flush path, MeteredKeyValueStore deserializes both newV=
alue and oldValue using the single headers set carried by the flush record.

=C2=A0

=C2=A0However, the flush record is built from:
 * newValue bytes from the cache
 * oldValue bytes read from the underlying store
 * one Record.headers() from the cached entry being flushed
 * =C2=A0

This means oldValue bytes and newValue bytes are not guaranteed to have bee=
n serialized with the same headers.

=C2=A0

When sendOldValues=3Dtrue and a header-aware serde is used, oldValue may th=
erefore be deserialized with headers that differ from the headers originall=
y used to serialize it.

=C2=A0

=C2=A0 As a result:
 * oldValue may be reconstructed incorrectly, or
 * deserialization may fail with an exception, depending on the serde imple=
mentation.

=C2=A0

=C2=A0 {*}Affected Stores{*}{*}{*}
 * MeteredWindowStore
 * MeteredKeyValueStore
 * MeteredSessionStore

=C2=A0

=C2=A0 {*}Why this happens{*}{*}{*}

=C2=A0 CachingKeyValueStore creates the flush record by:
 # taking newValue bytes from the cache
 # reading oldValue bytes from underlying store.
 # attaching entry.entry().context().headers() to the flush Record

=C2=A0

=C2=A0MeteredKeyValueStore.setFlushListener then deserializes both change.n=
ewValue and change.oldValue using *record.headers().*

=C2=A0

=C2=A0 {*}Condition{*}{*}{*}

=C2=A0 This issue requires:
 * caching enabled
 * sendOldValues=3Dtrue
 * a serde whose deserialize(topic, headers, bytes) behavior depends on hea=
ders

=C2=A0

{*}Expected behavior{*}{*}{*}

The cached flush path should not deserialize oldValue with flush-record hea=
ders unless those headers are guaranteed to match the headers originally us=
ed to serialize oldValue.



