Hello all,

Thanks for all feedbacks,

I believe we can simplify the approach further. Instead of comparing =
group creation=20
time against partition creation time to decide between EARLIEST and =
LATEST, we=20
can just always use the group creation timestamp to issue a =
ListOffsetsRequest for=20
all partitions =E2=80=94 whether there is no committed offset or the =
committed offset is out of=20
range. If the partition was created after the group, the lookup will =
naturally resolve to=20
the beginning of that partition. If the partition predates the group, =
the lookup will find the=20
first message at or after the group's creation time. Even for the =
out-of-range case (e.g.,=20
AdminClient.deleteRecords or log segment deletion), the same rule =
applies.=20

This approach is also more semantically accurate. The name by_start_time =
implies=20
"start consuming from when my group started." Using the group creation =
timestamp=20
as the sole reference point for all scenarios is exactly what this name =
promises =E2=80=94=20
no hidden branching between EARLIEST and LATEST, no conditional =
comparisons.=20
The behavior directly reflects the name.

This eliminates all conditional logic =E2=80=94 the behavior becomes a =
single, predictable rule:=20
always seek to the group's creation time. This simplification also =
aligns well with the basic=20
rule proposed earlier in the discussion: the strategy only affects the =
initial startup decision,=20
and after startup, the behavior remains consistent =E2=80=94 we always =
try to consume as much=20
data as possible regardless of the scenario.

Additionally, this reduces the scope of the protocol change, since we no =
longer need to pass=20
partition creation timestamps in the heartbeat response. Only the group =
creation time is required.=20

There are two points still worth discussing: Server clock accuracy:=20

1, The broker's clock may have some drift, which could affect the =
precision of the timestamp-based=20
lookup. However, this is equally true for the previous approach that =
compared two server-side=20
timestamps, so it does not introduce a new concern.=20

2. User data timestamps: If producers set custom timestamps on their =
records that do not reflect real=20
time, the ListOffsetsRequest may return unexpected results. However, =
this is fundamentally a data=20
quality issue that users should be responsible for.


Best Regards,
Jiunn-Yang

